==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* [[philippick]] {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{etyl|la|en}} {{term|philippicus|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|φιλιππικος|sc=polytonic|lang=grc}}, from {{term|Φιλιππος||lover of horses|sc=polytonic|lang=grc}}, from {{term|φιλο-|sc=polytonic|lang=grc}} + {{term|ίππος||horse|sc=polytonic|lang=grc}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/fɪˈlɪpɪk/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Any of the discourses of [[w:Demosthenes|Demosthenes]] against [[w:Philip II of Macedon|Philip II of Macedon]], defending the liberty of Athens.
# {{context|by extension|lang=en}} Any [[tirade]] or [[declamation]] full of bitter condemnation.
#* {{quote-book |year=1811 |author=[[w:Jane Austen|Jane Austen]] |title=[[s:Sense and Sensibility|Sense and Sensibility]] |chapter=Volume II, Chapter XII (a.k.a. Chapter 34) |url=http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Sense_and_Sensibility/Chapter_34#242 |page=234 |passage=Mrs. Ferrars looked exceedingly angry, and drawing herself up more stiffly than ever, pronounced in retort this bitter '''phillipic'''; “Miss Morton is Lord Morton’s daughter.”}}
#* {{quote-book|year=1922|passage=Skin-the-Goat, assuming he was he, evidently with an axe to grind, was airing his grievances in a forcible-feeble '''philippic''' anent the natural resources of Ireland, or something of that sort, which he described in his lengthy dissertation as the richest country bar none on the face of God’s earth, far and away superior to England|author=James Joyce|title=Ulysses}}

